  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 035a2531ee473eb48b75e12ca5d634d6, type: 3}
  m_Name: COC1_L1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::LessonData
  lessonID: 1
  lessonTitle: OHS Policies and Procedures
  description: "\tThis lesson introduces Occupational Health and Safety (OHS) practices
    to help protect workers, others, and equipment from potential hazards."
  lessonSceneName: LessonScene
  requiredGoldLessonID: 0
  learningObjectives:
  - "\u2022 Discuss the Occupational Health and Safety (OHS) policies and procedures.\n"
  - "\u2022 Elaborate the three steps used to manage health and safety at work.\n"
  - "\u2022 Point-out the common hazards encountered by computer technicians and
    users.\n"
  - "\u2022 Identify the elements of the Occupational Health and Safety Standards.\n"
  - "\u2022 Explain the 5S Principles.\n"
  slides:
  - slideType: 0
    content: "\tOccupational Safety and Health (OSH) is a planned system of working
      to prevent illness and injury where you work by recognizing and identifying
      hazards and risks.\n\nThere are three steps used to manage health and safety
      at work:\n\n1. Spot the Hazard (Hazard Identification)\n\n2. Assess the Risk
      (Risk Assessment)\n\n3. Make the Changes (Risk Control)\n\nAt work you can
      use these three Think Safe steps to help prevent accidents. Using the Think
      Safe Steps."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "\tA hazard is anything that could hurt you or someone else. Examples
      of workplace hazards include:\n\n\u2022 frayed electrical cords (could result
      in electrical shock)\n\n\u2022 boxes stacked precariously (they could fall
      on someone)\n\n\u2022 noisy machinery (could result in damage to your hearing)\n"
    image: {fileID: -1379164851, guid: 26accc8416e9f68419becb7f4528ad39, type: 3}
  - slideType: 0
    content: "\tDuring work experience, you must remain alert to anything that may
      be dangerous. If you see, hear or smell anything odd, take note. If you think
      it could be a hazard, tell someone. \n\n\t"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - slideType: 0
    content: "\tAssessing the risk means working out how likely it is that a hazard
      will harm someone and how serious the harm could be. Whenever you spot a hazard,
      assess the risk by asking yourself two questions:\n\n\u2022 How likely is it
      that the hazard could harm me or someone else?\n\n\u2022 How badly could I
      or someone else be harmed?\n\nIf you are not sure of the safest way to do something
      during work experience, always ask your work experience supervisor."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Mechanical Hazard\n\t\n\tWhen working on electronic equipment, always
      ask, \u201CCould this equipment hurt me?\u201D Be alert for moving parts, hot
      components, and sharp edges that could cause injury."
    image: {fileID: 852546510, guid: 2eb36148423c36049a41bad6c14f2806, type: 3}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Physical Hazard\n\n\tOne common hazard for computer technicians is
      cables across the floor. Someone could trip, fall, and get injured, creating
      a legal negligence issue. If a cable must cross the floor temporarily, place
      a Danger sign to warn people."
    image: {fileID: 397530054, guid: 0165cc06b85e5a34bb2d324f851d700b, type: 3}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Chemical Hazard\n\n\tThere are many chemicals used with electronic
      equipment, such as cleaners, compressed air, and solvents. Some can be harmful
      if swallowed or exposed to the skin or eyes. Always read the label and follow
      safety instructions. Be careful with printer ink and toner, as they can stain
      skin, clothing, and carpets."
    image: {fileID: 221726296, guid: 42e4763059ae8ca46aa4ee77249e7263, type: 3}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Electric Shock Hazard\n\n\tInside computers, most voltages are low,
      but the power supply can contain lethal 220V line voltage. Always unplug equipment
      before working inside. If you must work while it is powered, remove all jewelry
      and watches to reduce the risk of electrical injury."
    image: {fileID: -813718166, guid: 8578f1bc93ea4cd48a5520dd7c525dd8, type: 3}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Occupational Health and Safety Standards\n\n\tEveryone is responsible
      for identifying, addressing, and reporting workplace hazards to help maintain
      a safe environment."
    image: {fileID: 1060451888, guid: 6a567d5d88d6fc84bba860d7f3334f10, type: 3}
  - slideType: 0
    content: "\u2022 Identify the hazard.\n\n\u2022Clear the area near the hazard.\n\n\u2022Isolate
      or mark the hazard to protect others.\n\n\u2022Safely remove the hazard if
      possible.\n\n\u2022Report the hazard to the appropriate person if it cannot
      be safely removed.\n\n\u2022Complete the required report after the hazard is
      addressed."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Accident Reports\n\n\tAccident reports document specific details about
      incidents that occur during laboratory activities.\t"
    image: {fileID: -763154279, guid: 750da39e79467a44482bf4a4aa04cb91, type: 3}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Accident Reports\n\n\t\u2022 Name of the person injured\n\r\n\t\u2022
      Date and time of the accident\n\r\n\t\u2022 Type of injury\n\r\n\t\u2022 First
      aid given\n\r\n\t\u2022 Action taken to prevent further accidents\t"
    image: {fileID: -763154279, guid: 750da39e79467a44482bf4a4aa04cb91, type: 3}
  - slideType: 1
    content: "Fire Exits\n\n\tKeep fire exits and corridors clear of obstacles to
      ensure safe and easy evacuation during emergencies."
    image: {fileID: -1422242266, guid: c2de2cdf689aeb8468030add18e16159, type: 3}
  - slideType: 0
    content: "Personal Safety While Working with PC\u2019s\n\n\u2022 Always ground
      yourself before touching any part of the computer\n\r\n\u2022 Do not work alone\n\r\n\u2022
      Be careful with tools that may cause short circuit\n\r\n\u2022 Replace only
      fuses with those proper ratings\n\r\n\u2022 Wear safety glasses for protection
      against sparks and metal fragmented\n\r\n\u2022 Use only grounded plugs and
      receptacles\n\r\n\u2022 Working area should have ventilations, trash can, fire
      exit and capable of being disinfected\n"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - slideType: 0
    content: "Personal Safety While Working with PC\u2019s\n\n\u2022 Keep one hand
      in your pocket when working live circuit.\n\r\n\u2022 Wear rubber sole shoes
      when standing on the ground or in a concrete floor.\n\r\n\u2022 Always power
      off and unplug the computer before working on it.\n\r\n\u2022 Wear hard hat
      when someone working above you\n\r\n\u2022 When making circuit changes, switch
      off and unplug the power cord from the equipment then discharge the capacitors."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  arActivity: {fileID: 0}
  quizData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 60a7df15bc161704e98efbe014e68758, type: 2}
